    pr_ref:  # 77dc
    description: "FOUND 2026-09-01 by WI-jinuj's mandatory report-only pass, which was run to\nsize a COLLECTOR widening and instead found that two of the three consumers\nthat widening was filed to reach are invisible for a different reason.\n\nTHE FACT. axis_drift.find_drift matches a consumer by SUBSTRING on its TARGET\nNAME. Two live io-boundary consumers do not contain \"BOUNDAR\":\n\n    taint.py:1540         AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP     dict[io-boundary, taint_label]\n    io_boundary.py:196    DEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS {\"net_listen\": \"net_recv\"}\n\nBoth are keyed (and DEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS also valued) by io-boundary\nnames. Both branch on the vocabulary. Neither can EVER be reached by the\nio-boundary drift linter, no matter how the value-shape collector is widened\n-- WI-jinuj widened it to dicts and confirmed the scan still does not see\nthem. WI-jinuj's own filed text attributes their invisibility to the\ncollector; that attribution is wrong and this item records the correction.\n\nWHY THE FILTER EXISTS, so the fix is not a naive removal: the substring test\nis what stops the scan reporting every unrelated string set in the tree that\nhappens to share a value with the registry -- language keyword vocabularies,\nstdlib method-name catalogues. Dropping it turns four linters into noise\ngenerators. The docstring on iter_axis_set_assignments says so.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE PROBLEM. The filter conflates two questions: \"is this\nconstant ABOUT the axis?\" (what we want) and \"is the axis's name spelled in\nthis identifier?\" (what is tested). A consumer named for what it MAPS TO\nrather than what it maps FROM -- AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP is named for the taint\nlabel, DEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS for the shadowing relation -- is about the\naxis while never naming it.\n\nOPTIONS, none costed:\n  (a) An explicit per-axis INCLUDED_TARGET_NAMES allowlist beside the existing\n      excluded_target_names. Cheap, exact, and it does not weaken the filter;\n      the cost is that it only ever contains what someone remembered to add,\n      which is the same class of gap as the one being fixed.\n  (b) A second pass keyed on VALUE membership rather than name -- collect every\n      module-level string collection and report only those whose values are a\n      non-trivial SUBSET of the registry. Self-maintaining, and the \"non-trivial\"\n      threshold is where the false positives live; needs measurement before it\n      is worth anything.\n  (c) Rename the two constants to contain the axis substring. Cheapest to\n      implement, worst as a rule: it makes a linter's reach depend on\n      identifier fashion, and the next consumer will be named badly again.\n\nMEASURE FIRST, and the measurement is cheap: run option (b) in report-only\nmode over all four axes at several subset thresholds and count how many\nnon-registry constants it would drag in. If (b) is quiet, it dominates (a);\nif it is noisy, (a) is the honest answer and the residual gap gets written\ndown rather than closed.\n\nNOT KNOWN: whether the two named constants have actually DRIFTED today. They\nhave never been checked, which is the point -- their correctness is currently\nan accident rather than a gate. Check them as step zero; a drift found there\nis a separate bug and should be filed as one."  # 77dc
    fields:  # 77dc
      statement: "axis_drift.find_drift selects consumers by SUBSTRING match on the target identifier. taint.AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP and io_boundary.DEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS are both keyed by io-boundary names and both branch on that vocabulary, but neither identifier contains 'BOUNDAR', so the io-boundary drift linter can never reach them regardless of how the value-shape collector is widened. Confirmed after WI-jinuj widened the collector to dicts: the BOUNDAR-filtered scan still does not see either constant."  # 77dc
      root_cause: "The substring filter conflates 'is this constant ABOUT the axis' with 'is the axis name spelled in this identifier'. A consumer named for what it maps TO rather than what it maps FROM is about the axis while never naming it. The filter cannot simply be dropped -- it is what stops the scan reporting unrelated string sets that share a value with the registry by coincidence. WI-jinuj's filed text attributes these two constants' invisibility to the collector shape; that attribution is wrong and is corrected here."  # 77dc
